  "question": {
    "text": "An unknown alcohol is treated with the &quot;Lucas reagent&quot; to determine whether the alcohol is primary, secondary or tertiary. Which alcohol reacts fastest and by what mechanism:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mt4iH",
      "text": "Secondary alcohol by S<sub>N</sub>2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "K2h10",
      "text": "Tertiary alcohol by S<sub>N</sub>2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "wS6kP",
      "text": "Secondary alcohol by S<sub>N</sub>1",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "kbXxP",
      "text": "Tertiary alcohol by S<sub>N</sub>1",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>3&deg;&nbsp;alcohol reacts fastest giving white turbidity immediately on adding Lucas reagent.<br />Reaction takes place through carbocation formation involving S<sub>N</sub><sup>1</sup>&nbsp;mechanism.&nbsp;Rate of reaction in this reaction is directly proportional to the stability of the carbocation formed.<br />3&deg;&nbsp;R-OH(tertiary alcohol) forms 3&deg;&nbsp;carbocation and therefore, the&nbsp;reaction is fastest.<br /><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/389d19f9-0233-4330-a80a-0b91361c4042-image.png\" style=\"width: 648px; height: 170px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
